// The reading library for the Expectant Father Companion.
// Each read is a summary in our own words of a passage in Brott and Ash Rudick, The Expectant Father, 5th ed.
// topic: her-body | food | baby | you | money-work | gear | birth | after | after-prep | appointments
// months: the months of pregnancy in which it is offered as a daily read (1 to 9). Omit to offer always.
window.READS = [

// ---------- Deciding where and with whom ----------
{id:"vote", topic:"you", months:[2,3], title:"Give her the bigger vote", text:"Where the baby is born, who attends and how pain is handled are joint decisions, but not equal ones. The book's rule of thumb is that she holds the deciding share, a little over half, because every option lands on her body far more than on yours. Your job is to research, turn up, ask the awkward questions and have an opinion. Then let her settle it. The same question returns at every stage, from the scan schedule to pain relief on the day, so the habit is worth building early."},
{id:"hospital-gives", topic:"birth", months:[2,4], title:"What a hospital birth gives you", text:"Most first-time parents deliver in hospital, and the main reason is backup. If something goes wrong, specialists and equipment are already in the building, and in the first day or two the staff watch mother and baby and field your hundred small questions. The trade is privacy and control. Expect routine checks, rules that may feel arbitrary, and a bedroom-styled room that still feels like a ward. Tour what is available to you, and remember the room you like may be allocated on the day."},
{id:"hospital-only", topic:"birth", months:[2,4], title:"When hospital is the only choice", text:"Some situations close the discussion about where to give birth. If she is carrying more than one baby, is over 35 or under 17, had trouble in a previous labour, has developed complications this time, or has a condition such as high blood pressure, diabetes, a heart or bleeding disorder, kidney disease or epilepsy, the book is firm that hospital is the right setting. The same applies if she smokes or has had a previous C-section. If her doctor has used the words high risk, treat that as the end of the home-birth conversation."},
{id:"pain-not-test", topic:"birth", months:[3,8], title:"Pain relief is not a test", text:"The book prefers the word unmedicated to natural, because all births are natural. Its point is that some birth methods treat pain relief as failure, and that attitude makes new parents feel bad at the moment they should be celebrating. In the US more than 70 percent of women use an epidural or spinal, a figure that will not match the Maldives. The decision is hers, you join the discussion, and the plan stays flexible. A hoped-for drug-free birth may need intervention, and a planned epidural may be unavailable when the anaesthetist is elsewhere."},
{id:"on-call", topic:"appointments", months:[2,6], title:"Who is on call that night", text:"Over 90 percent of fathers now attend the birth, so you will spend a lot of time with whoever is caring for her, and you should be comfortable with them too. The thing most couples miss is that doctors work in groups. The person she sees at every check may not be the one on duty when labour starts. Ask early how the rota works, how often the regular doctor attends their own patients' births, and who the backups are. Meeting them beforehand saves you a stranger in the room on a stressful night."},
{id:"forgotten-qs", topic:"appointments", months:[2,6], title:"Questions most couples forget", text:"Beyond the usual questions about hospital and philosophy, the book suggests some you may not think of. What is this practitioner's C-section rate and how do they decide to operate. Will they wait for the cord to stop pulsing before clamping it. Can the lights be dimmed and can the baby go straight to her chest. Can she walk and shower in early labour. Can you cut the cord, or sit near her shoulders if surgery is needed. Is there an advice line for when you panic at 2am. Book a separate appointment for this, because a routine visit is too short."},
{id:"midwife", topic:"birth", months:[2,5], title:"What a midwife can do", text:"Midwives are the norm in much of the world and increasingly common alongside an obstetrician. The book describes trained midwives as specialists in uncomplicated, low-risk birth who work with a doctor in the background in case something changes. They cannot perform surgery or use forceps, and they usually take low-risk cases only. If you are considering one, ask about licensing, how many births they have attended, and how and how often they transfer care to a doctor."},
{id:"doula-1", topic:"birth", months:[5,8], title:"Doulas, support without medicine", text:"A doula is a trained, non-medical companion who supports the labouring woman and her partner through the birth. The book cites several studies linking doulas with shorter labours and fewer C-sections. It also warns that doulas are unregulated and that some hospitals have banned them after clashes with medical staff. The author's view is coloured by his own experience and his book on the subject. Whether any exist where she will deliver is unknown, so ask the hospital before you look further."},
{id:"bills", topic:"money-work", months:[7,9], title:"Read every bill carefully", text:"The book's advice on bills translates anywhere: check them line by line. It cites a credit agency study finding errors in nine out of ten hospital bills, almost never in the patient's favour. That is a US figure and probably dated, but the categories of mistake are universal. Look for double charges, a private room billed when she had a shared one, brand-name drugs when generics were given, and procedures that never happened. The author's own review of his second child's bills cut the total by about a fifth."},

// ---------- Her body and the first months ----------
{id:"first-weeks-inside", topic:"her-body", months:[2,2], title:"Her first weeks, inside", text:"Before anything shows, her body is already busy. Expect some mixture of nausea, heartburn, cravings or sudden dislikes, dizziness, headaches, irritability, tender and enlarging breasts, and a tiredness that can flatten her by mid afternoon. Emotionally she may be thrilled, stunned, scared and occasionally miserable, sometimes all in one hour. Many women also feel a new closeness to their partner alongside apprehension about the months ahead. None of this needs fixing. It needs noticing, and a partner who does not take the moods personally."},
{id:"one-cell", topic:"baby", months:[2,2], title:"From one cell to a heartbeat", text:"Within a day of conception one sperm out of tens of millions fertilised the egg in the fallopian tube. By the end of that first day the single cell had split in two, and over the following days the cluster drifted down into the uterus and embedded in its wall, where it will stay until June. By the end of the first month it is about six millimetres long, roughly ten thousand times its starting size, with a beating heart, no brain yet, and small buds where arms and legs will grow."},
{id:"thrill-relief", topic:"you", months:[2,2], title:"The thrill, and the relief", text:"The author describes the morning of the positive test as the nearest thing to winning a jackpot, even though the result was not a surprise. Underneath the excitement he found two feelings he had not expected. One was relief, because he had quietly feared for years that he might be infertile. The other was a surge of pride, a slightly embarrassing sense of having proven himself. Both are common and neither is shameful. Notice what you actually felt when the line appeared, because it tells you something about what you have been carrying."},
{id:"strange-doubt", topic:"you", months:[2,3], title:"The strange doubt that passes", text:"After the first excitement, a surprising number of men get a fleeting, irrational thought that the baby might not be theirs. The book cites one psychologist's interviews with about two hundred expectant fathers, of whom 60 percent admitted passing doubts of this kind. Almost none actually suspected their partner. The explanation offered is insecurity rather than jealousy, a quiet disbelief that you could be part of making a life. This rests on a single, older study, so hold the number loosely. The useful part is knowing the thought is common and usually gone within weeks."},
{id:"nausea-lands", topic:"you", months:[2,3], title:"Her nausea lands on you", text:"Morning sickness is her burden, but the book flags a small Australian study showing it hits partners too. Men described disrupted work, frustration and helplessness at being unable to relieve her symptoms, and worry about whether the baby was developing properly. A few admitted feeling manipulated, a thought the author advises keeping to yourself. For most women the nausea starts four to six weeks after conception and lifts by fourteen or fifteen weeks, roughly mid December for you. If it continues past that or she is losing weight, see her doctor."},
{id:"keep-moving", topic:"her-body", months:[2,8], title:"Why she should keep moving", text:"Exercise in pregnancy is not optional. The American obstetricians' body recommends 150 minutes a week of moderate activity for healthy pregnant women. The book lists steadier weight gain, better sleep and mood, lower gestational diabetes risk and fewer aches, plus strength and stamina for labour and a quicker recovery afterwards. One Spanish study found women who exercised three times a week had labours about an hour shorter. That is a single study. If she was active before, she can usually continue with her doctor's approval."},
{id:"train-together", topic:"her-body", months:[2,8], title:"Train together, gently", text:"The easiest way to get her exercising when she feels wrecked is to do it with her, and the book's list suits where you live. Walking at any pace, swimming, water aerobics and snorkelling all appear, along with cycling on smooth roads, light weights and yoga without extreme stretches, because her connective tissue loosens in pregnancy. Start easy and stop when she tires. Regular beats heroic: about thirty minutes most days. Both of you should drink a glass of water an hour before and a few mouthfuls every fifteen to twenty minutes while moving."},
{id:"not-do", topic:"her-body", months:[2,9], title:"What she should not do", text:"The book's no list is short and specific. Scuba diving is out for the whole pregnancy because the fetus cannot decompress, which matters in the Maldives. Avoid anything with a real risk of a hard fall, such as horse riding, skating and, from about the seventh month, cycling. Contact sports, heavy lifting and overheating are out. A simple gauge: if she cannot hold a conversation while exercising, she is pushing too hard. For the first six to eight weeks keep her core temperature below about 39°C, so no hot tubs, saunas or steam rooms, and go easy in the midday heat."},
{id:"past", topic:"you", months:[2,3], title:"Do not relitigate the past", text:"Almost every couple discovers the pregnancy after she has already done something on the avoid list: a dive, a sauna, a few drinks, a bout of heavy lifting. The book is blunt about this. Nothing can be undone now, torturing yourselves changes nothing, and the odds that any of it affected the baby are slim. The same applies to things you may have done, like smoking around her. Mention anything that worries you to her doctor at the next visit and then let it go."},
{id:"smoking", topic:"her-body", months:[2,4], title:"Smoking, including yours", text:"Her smoking fills the womb with carbon monoxide and nicotine, and even one cigarette a day raises the risk of miscarriage, early birth and low birth weight and doubles the risk of cot death. The book is equally hard on fathers. Your smoking during pregnancy exposes her and the baby to second-hand smoke, with Swedish research linking it to birth defects and childhood leukaemia. Vaping is described as less toxic but not safe, because nicotine can reduce blood flow to the placenta. If either of you smokes, quit now."},
{id:"medicines-fasting", topic:"her-body", months:[2,5], title:"Medicines and going hungry", text:"Two rules for the medicine cabinet and the kitchen. She should check with her doctor before any medication, including aspirin, ibuprofen and cold remedies, especially anything with alcohol or codeine. The book cites one study linking paracetamol in the third trimester to developmental problems, which is a single, contested finding, and notes the evidence on antidepressants cuts both ways, so untreated depression must be weighed too. On food, it says she should never go 24 hours without eating without a doctor's approval, especially before week nineteen. Ramadan 2027 falls in her second trimester, so raise fasting with her doctor early."},
{id:"cats-gardens", topic:"her-body", months:[2,4], title:"Cats, gardens and chemicals", text:"The toxoplasma parasite in raw meat also lives in cat faeces, so for the duration you own the litter tray. The book adds that garden soil is the more likely contact point, since outdoor cats use it freely, so gardening with gloves becomes yours too. Remove chemical pesticides, weed killers and cockroach sprays from the house, because repeated exposure has been linked to birth defects, and keep her away from flea collars, pest strips and insect bombs. Hair dye is considered low risk, but the cautious advice is to wait until the second trimester and choose ammonia-free products."},
{id:"month4-body", topic:"her-body", months:[4,4], title:"Her body and mood in month four", text:"The fourth month, roughly December into January for you, brings a cluster of changes. Her nipples darken and freckles and moles may deepen, which is pigmentation and normal. Her appetite returns, for food and for sex. She may become clumsy, dropping and spilling things. Her vision can shift and contact lenses may bother her. Gums may swell and bleed, which affects most pregnant women. Emotionally the miscarriage fear eases after the scan, replaced by wondering what being a mother will mean, more forgetfulness, more need to hear that you love her, and real distress when clothes stop fitting."},
{id:"practice-contractions", topic:"her-body", months:[5,7], title:"Practice contractions and the dark line", text:"From around the fifth month she may notice her belly tightening now and then, without pain. These are practice contractions. The difference from the real thing is simple: in true labour the cervix opens, in practice runs it does not. Expect them to become more frequent by the seventh month. Around the same time a dark vertical line can appear from her navel downwards, her nipples darken further, her navel may pop outward, and her breasts may leak a little. Fluid retention can even change the shape of her eyes, so contact lenses may stop fitting for a while."},
{id:"month6-body", topic:"her-body", months:[6,6], title:"Her body in month six", text:"This is when weight goes on fastest. Her heart and lungs are working about half again as hard as before pregnancy, so breathlessness, sweating and dizziness are common. Extra fluid presses on nerves, producing tingling hands, sciatica or carpal tunnel symptoms, and her hands and feet swell. A nagging backache sets in. She may leak a little urine when she laughs or sneezes. Emotionally the moods are steadier, but she may feel the pregnancy will never end, and she may start asking what kind of mother she will be and how her own mother shaped her."},
{id:"memory", topic:"her-body", months:[4,9], title:"Pregnancy and her memory", text:"If she is losing keys and words, she is in good company. One anaesthetist reported that women's brains shrink by 3 to 5 percent in pregnancy, and an Australian review of twenty studies found poorer memory and concentration, especially in the last trimester. Both findings are disputed, and other researchers blame exhaustion and broken sleep, which affect memory in much the same way. Either way it reverses within months of the birth. None of this is information to share with her."},
{id:"month7-body", topic:"her-body", months:[7,7], title:"Her body in month seven", text:"Comfort declines. Heartburn, gas, constipation, cramps and dizziness are all common, her face may puff and her belly itch. Her hip joints are loosening, which changes how she walks and makes her clumsier and more prone to pulled muscles. A thick white discharge is normal. Practice contractions grow more frequent. Emotionally she may be dreaming vividly about the baby, worrying whether she will have the energy to return to work, and starting to fear the labour itself. Watch for a sudden urge to get the house ready. That is the first sign of nesting, and it can tip into feeling overwhelmed by the list."},

// ---------- Food and nutrition ----------
{id:"how-much-more", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"How much more she eats", text:"Eating for two is a myth in the first trimester. If she was well nourished before, the book says she needs no extra calories at all until around mid December, because the growing baby takes what it needs from a normal diet. From the second trimester add roughly 340 calories a day, and in the third, from late March, about 450. Twins or a low starting weight change the sums. If she was overweight before, this is not the time to diet either. The research the book cites suggests what she eats now shapes the child's lifelong risk of diabetes, heart disease and obesity."},
{id:"calcium", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Calcium, and where to find it", text:"The baby's bones are built from her calcium, and if the supply runs short her own bones pay for it, raising her later risk of osteoporosis. The target is at least 1,000 mg a day. Dairy is the obvious route, but many Asian women are lactose intolerant and the book notes doctors may steer those women away from milk. Alternatives it lists include tinned salmon with the soft bones, tofu, dark leafy greens, broccoli, eggs and fortified juice. Vitamin D and magnesium both help her absorb it, so ask her doctor whether she needs either."},
{id:"iron", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Iron, nearly double", text:"She now needs about 27 mg of iron a day, almost twice her previous requirement, because so much of it is going into the baby's blood. Fall short and she becomes anaemic and exhausted. The book suggests three servings of iron-rich food daily: spinach, dried fruit, lean meat or poultry, fortified cereals and pulses. Food alone is often not enough, and her doctor may add a supplement, usually after the third month. Taking tablets with orange juice or another vitamin C source helps absorption. Iron tablets commonly cause constipation, which is one reason fibre matters."},
{id:"protein", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Protein for a growing brain", text:"A non-pregnant woman needs around 45 g of protein a day. The book puts her target now at 70 to 100 g. The reason is brain building: at eight weeks the fetus has about 125,000 neurons, then production accelerates to roughly a thousand a second, reaching more than 25 billion by week nineteen, which for you is late January. Many nutritionists think a protein-rich diet through those weeks supports that growth. Lean sources are best: skinless chicken, fish chosen with care, tofu, peanut butter, low-fat cheese and cooked eggs. A glass of milk gives about 8 g."},
{id:"small-nutrients", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"The smaller nutrients", text:"Beyond the headline minerals, the book names a few quieter ones. Choline, about 450 mg a day, supports brain development and is found in beef, eggs, milk, peanuts and soy, though it is hard to get enough from food and is usually in antenatal supplements. Magnesium helps calcium absorption and a shortfall may affect fetal brain development or trigger early labour. Vitamin C builds collagen and the baby's bones and teeth. Vitamins A and B from green and yellow vegetables help her body use all that extra protein. Her doctor decides on supplements, your job is the shopping."},
{id:"colour", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Seven servings of colour", text:"The book's rule for fruit and vegetables is variety of colour, aiming for at least seven servings a day between them. Green and yellow vegetables, a group that oddly includes mango and melon, supply iron, folate and vitamins A and B. The darker the green, the better. Fruit brings vitamin C and antioxidants. This is one area where you can do most of the work: a fruit bowl that is always full, cut vegetables in the fridge, and a side of greens with whatever is for dinner. Local papaya, mango, watermelon and leafy greens all count."},
{id:"grains-fibre", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Grains, fibre and constipation", text:"She should have at least four servings of grains a day, because her body burns that fuel first and the baby gets what is left. The book favours whole grains over white bread and white rice for a few months, plus brown rice, potatoes, peas, dried beans and quinoa. The practical argument is fibre. Iron supplements constipate, and pregnancy itself slows digestion, so fibre and water together are the defence. In a rice-based household this is the hardest switch, so negotiate rather than impose, perhaps mixing brown and white rice to start."},
{id:"water", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Water, more than you think", text:"The book's target is eight to twelve glasses of water or unsweetened, caffeine-free fluid a day, and more if she exercises or is pregnant through hot weather, which in the Maldives is always. She sweats more in pregnancy and needs the fluid to carry waste away. Being well hydrated eases headaches, bloating and constipation. The simplest check is the colour of her urine, which should be nearly clear or pale yellow. Dark means she is already behind. Keep a bottle by the bed, one in the bag and one in the car, and refill them yourself."},
{id:"fats", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Fats, the right kind", text:"She needs fat, but the book suggests keeping it to about 30 percent of calories, with most of it from plants: nuts, olive oil, avocado. Animal fats like butter and ghee should be limited. One Danish researcher the book cites argues the case against saturated fat has been overstated, but that is one voice and the advice remains to limit it. The firm rule is trans fats: avoid anything whose ingredients list says hydrogenated or partially hydrogenated. Nine calories per gram makes fat the easiest way to overshoot."},
{id:"alcohol-caffeine", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Alcohol, caffeine and other drugs", text:"On alcohol the book's position is that complete abstinence is safest, that heavy drinking causes irreversible harm, and that even moderate social drinking has been linked to low birth weight and early miscarriage. It quotes one obstetrician saying an occasional glass is probably fine but admits there is no data to prove it. Mainstream guidance is no known safe amount. Caffeine is gentler: a cup or two of coffee a day appears acceptable, more than three or four has been linked to miscarriage and early birth. Recreational drugs, including cannabis, are a flat no."},
{id:"food-safety", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Food safety beyond the obvious", text:"Three infections and one metal drive the food rules. Raw or undercooked meat and fish can carry toxoplasma, which can damage the fetus's eyes or nervous system. Unpasteurised milk and soft cheeses such as brie can carry listeria. Raw eggs and undercooked chicken can carry salmonella. Practitioners disagree on how big the risk is, and the author's wife's own obstetrician allowed sushi. Mercury is the other concern: avoid shark, swordfish, tilefish and large mackerel, and the book limits albacore tuna to one or two servings a week. Ask her doctor about local tuna and reef fish specifically."},
{id:"kitchen", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"What to keep in the kitchen", text:"The book's pantry list is built so that either of you can make something decent in ten minutes with no planning. Its spine is wholegrain bread, pasta and cereal, rice, tinned beans and tuna, eggs with a few already boiled, natural peanut butter, yoghurt, oil, vinegar and spices. Add vegetables that can be eaten raw, fresh and frozen fruit, dried fruit, crackers and plenty of water. The author also allows the occasional doughnut. Translate it to what you can actually buy: local fish, lentils, dhal, bananas and papaya fill the same roles."},
{id:"labels", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Reading the small print", text:"Packaging is designed to mislead, and you are now the one reading it. Ingredients are listed by weight, so if the healthy grain on the front of the box is near the bottom, put it back. Sugar hides under many names: fructose, corn syrup, dextrose, cane syrup, malt syrup, honey. Anything called a fruit drink or cocktail may be as little as a tenth juice. Check the serving size against what a person actually eats, because a small serving makes the numbers look better. Light, low fat and natural often mean the fat was swapped for sugar."},
{id:"vegetarian", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Vegetarian pregnancies work", text:"If she is vegetarian the book sees no problem, especially if she eats eggs and dairy, which cover protein, calcium and choline between them. A strict vegan diet needs more deliberate planning to hit the protein target and the smaller nutrients, and the book's advice is a conversation with her doctor or a nutritionist rather than guesswork. The practical fallout is yours: pulses, tofu, nuts, fortified foods and dark greens need to be in the kitchen in quantity. This section is thin in the book, so treat it as a prompt to ask rather than a complete answer."},
{id:"police-plate", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Do not police her plate", text:"Helping her eat well is one of the most useful things you can do, but the book draws a line at hovering. Being pregnant is hard enough without someone auditing every bite, and an occasional portion of chips or a chocolate bar does no lasting harm. It even notes studies linking dark chocolate to lower stress, and one to a lower rate of pre-eclampsia, which is a single correlational study. The real rule is to eat the way you are asking her to eat, and keep your indulgences private."},
{id:"hunger", topic:"food", months:[2,9], title:"Feed the hunger before it hits", text:"The author says he kept underestimating how fast and how hard his wife's hunger arrived. The fix is to take over the food logistics. Learn a handful of quick meals you can make in twenty minutes. Plan the week and write the shopping list as you go. Do the shopping yourself, both to spare her feet and because the smell of a full supermarket can be unbearable with nausea. Make her a breakfast shake so she can stay in bed a little longer. Keep nuts, raisins or a protein bar in the car, because her energy can drop without warning."},
{id:"baby-tastes", topic:"food", months:[5,9], title:"What she eats, the baby tastes", text:"The baby has been swallowing amniotic fluid for a month or two already, and the taste buds are now about as sensitive as yours. The flavour of that fluid shifts with your partner's meals. In one small study, adults could detect garlic in fluid sampled from women who had eaten it less than an hour earlier. The point for you is practical: the baby is building a memory of whatever your household cooks. After birth, newborns prefer the scent of their own mother and, when weaning starts, tend to favour the flavours she ate while pregnant."},

// ---------- Appointments and tests ----------
{id:"why-appts", topic:"appointments", months:[2,4], title:"Why her appointments matter to you", text:"Most research finds women feel the pregnancy is real well before men do. The exception is fathers who turn up from the start and keep turning up. Going to her antenatal visits does four things. It makes you a participant rather than someone watching from the side. It gives you the moments that make it concrete, the heartbeat around month three and the baby moving on screen around month five. It shows her, in a way flowers cannot, that she is not carrying this alone. And the staff start treating you as someone worth talking to. If daytime slots are hard, ask about early or late ones."},
{id:"appt-rhythm", topic:"appointments", months:[2,3], title:"The appointment rhythm, with dates", text:"For a single baby the usual pattern is one visit a month through the first six months, then every two weeks in months seven and eight, then weekly in the final month. With a due date around 13 June 2027, that means monthly visits until roughly the end of March, fortnightly through April and the first half of May, then weekly until the birth. Put the whole run in your calendar now and tell work early, rather than negotiating each visit as it comes. Clinics vary, so check the actual schedule with hers."},
{id:"taken-seriously", topic:"appointments", months:[2,5], title:"Getting staff to take you seriously", text:"Two small studies of fathers at antenatal visits, one American and one Australian, found the same thing. Men felt they were treated as a novelty, neither the patient nor quite a visitor, and were only addressed when the subject was how to support her. If that happens to you, the author's advice is blunt. Say from the first visit that you want to be involved, ask questions at every appointment, and keep asking until ignoring you becomes impossible. He also admits plenty of men make it easy to be sidelined by hovering in a corner. Pick a chair near the desk and use it."},
{id:"screen-vs-diagnose", topic:"appointments", months:[2,4], title:"Screening is not diagnosis", text:"The two words get used interchangeably but mean different things. A screen, such as a blood test or an ultrasound, estimates the chance of a problem. It cannot harm the baby and it cannot confirm anything. A diagnostic test confirms or rules out a condition, and the ones that do involve a needle and a small risk. So a screen that comes back positive is not a diagnosis. It means the odds are above a cut-off and further checks are offered. Most of these positives turn out fine. Before any screen, agree together what you would do with a worrying result."},
{id:"combined-screen", topic:"appointments", months:[2,3], title:"The combined first-trimester screen", text:"This is the main early screen and it has a tight window, weeks 11 to 14, which for you falls roughly between 22 November and mid December 2026. It has two parts done together. An ultrasound measures a pocket of fluid at the back of the baby's neck, since extra fluid there is associated with chromosomal conditions and some heart problems. A blood sample from her measures two pregnancy chemicals. Combined, the book puts detection at about 85 percent with a 5 percent false positive rate. Treat the accuracy figures as dated and ask the clinic for theirs."},
{id:"nipt", topic:"appointments", months:[2,3], title:"Cell-free DNA screening", text:"Fragments of the baby's DNA circulate in her blood, and a single blood draw from about week 9 or 10 can analyse them. The book calls this the biggest advance in prenatal testing. Its strength is ruling things out. A negative result is close to definitive and should let you both stop worrying. A positive still needs confirming with amniocentesis or CVS, because false positives happen. The field has moved on since the edition was written, so ask the clinic what their test detects and what it costs locally. The window opens in early November."},
{id:"second-screen", topic:"appointments", months:[4,5], title:"The second-trimester blood screen", text:"Between weeks 15 and 20, roughly 20 December to the end of January for you, her blood can be tested for three, four or five markers. The triple, quad and penta names simply count how many chemicals are measured, and in theory more markers mean fewer false alarms. The screen flags the chance of Down syndrome, trisomy 18 and neural tube conditions. Results come within a week. The book makes one practical point. This screen exists to decide whether more testing is needed, so if she already plans a detailed scan or an amniocentesis, she may reasonably skip it."},
{id:"amnio-cvs", topic:"appointments", months:[3,5], title:"Amniocentesis and CVS compared", text:"These are the two tests that confirm rather than estimate. Amniocentesis is done around weeks 15 to 18. A fine needle goes through her abdomen into the sac and draws a small amount of fluid, with results in one to three weeks. It picks up nearly every chromosomal condition but not structural ones such as a cleft palate. The old miscarriage figure of 1 in 200 is outdated, and the book puts current risk far lower. CVS samples placental tissue at weeks 9 to 12, carries slightly more risk, and its only real advantage is being earlier. Neither is routine without a reason."},
{id:"scans", topic:"appointments", months:[2,5], title:"What each scan is looking for", text:"Ultrasound is painless for her and safe for the baby, and it can be done from about week five. Early on, most doctors only scan if something needs checking, such as the uterus measuring differently from the dates, any bleeding, a question over how many babies there are, or concern about an ectopic pregnancy. The first-trimester scan can confirm a heartbeat and measure the baby from crown to rump, which sharpens the due date. The mid-pregnancy scan counts fingers and toes, watches movement, checks the placenta, examines brain and spine, confirms organs are the right size and place, and can reveal the sex if you want to know."},
{id:"your-blood", topic:"you", months:[2,4], title:"Blood tests that involve you", text:"You are not the patient, but you may still be asked for blood. Some inherited conditions run more commonly in certain populations, and the book lists thalassaemia as one that affects families of Asian, South Asian and Middle Eastern origin, which includes much of the Maldives. If neither of you has been checked as a carrier, ask. The other case is rhesus. If her blood is Rh negative and yours is positive, as it is for about 85 percent of men, her immune system could react to the baby's blood. The fix is a course of injections for her starting around week 28, late March 2027."},
{id:"pelvic-exam", topic:"appointments", months:[2,4], title:"The pelvic exam, and asking to look", text:"Internal examinations are routine and you will get used to them, but the first few can feel strange, especially if her doctor is a man. The author describes being invited to look at his wife's cervix and finding that it made the whole process less mysterious and more his own, while admitting it was odd. If that interests you, two rules. Ask her first, in private, and accept that she may feel it is intrusive. Then ask the doctor, and expect a surprised look. If she says no, that is the end of it."},
{id:"your-doctor", topic:"you", months:[3,6], title:"Your own doctor has a job too", text:"Studies the book cites found that men see their own doctor less often than usual while their partner is pregnant, because all the health worry gets pointed at her and the baby. That is backwards. You are about to need stamina, broken sleep and a steady head, and anything you have been putting off, a check-up, a dental visit, a nagging pain, is cheaper to deal with now than in July. And whatever aches you do have, the book's advice is to keep them to yourself while she is pregnant."},

// ---------- You: feelings and the relationship ----------
{id:"fussing", topic:"you", months:[4,6], title:"When protectiveness tips into fussing", text:"Around the fourth month many fathers become preoccupied with her safety and the baby's, quizzing her about protein, nagging about exercise, worrying about how she sleeps. The author admits he was a pain. His advice is to be gentle and ease off, because she is carrying the same worries. Some facts help. Lying flat on her back late in pregnancy can press on a large vein, but she will feel uncomfortable and shift long before any harm. Screens, keyboards and airport scanners are fine. Anything still bothering you goes on the list for the next appointment, not into a lecture at dinner."},
{id:"couvade-when", topic:"you", months:[3,9], title:"Couvade: when it comes and what it predicts", text:"Estimates for how many expectant fathers get physical symptoms vary wildly, with some American studies putting it as high as 90 percent, which tells you the definitions are loose. The timing is more consistent. Symptoms tend to appear around the third month, fade through the middle of pregnancy and return in the last month or two, then vanish at the birth. The range is wide: weight gain, nausea, cravings, headaches, toothache, poor concentration. The interesting finding is what they predict. Studies link stronger symptoms, and a bigger drop in testosterone, to more hands-on care of the newborn. Having none means nothing either way."},
{id:"your-hormones-preg", topic:"you", months:[3,9], title:"Your hormones are moving as well", text:"Her hormones rise through pregnancy, including prolactin, which prepares her to feed, and cortisol, which seems tied to bonding. One Canadian researcher took blood from both partners at several points and found fathers' prolactin and cortisol followed the same curve as their partners', in smaller swings. Anthropologists have measured testosterone in expectant fathers falling by up to a third around the birth, which one reads as the body shifting a man from courtship to care. These were small studies, and levels return to normal after the birth."},
{id:"why-symptoms", topic:"you", months:[3,9], title:"Why fathers get symptoms at all", text:"The book offers several explanations. Sympathy or guilt, because men are trained to absorb pain and she may remind you who got her into this. Jealousy, because she is getting the attention. Plain stress, with one researcher finding more symptoms in men whose pregnancies were unplanned or whose money was tight. And a speculative evolutionary one, that symptoms are a signal of commitment she can trust because a nosebleed is harder to fake than a promise. Deliberate versions go back two thousand years in various cultures. The common thread is men trying to stay inside the event."},
{id:"telling-family", topic:"you", months:[3,3], title:"Telling family first", text:"Unless there is a strong reason not to, family hears before friends. A friend will forgive learning it from your aunt, your aunt will not forgive the reverse. The exception the book gives is a sibling or close relative who has been trying for years without success, where a couple may choose to hold the news longer. If you are keeping it private for now, the leaks are rarely announcements. They are phrases like “she needs to rest” and sudden changes in her habits. Think about what friends will notice at the next gathering and have a dull explanation ready."},
{id:"telling-work", topic:"money-work", months:[3,4], title:"Telling work early enough", text:"The book suggests telling colleagues and your employer around the time you tell friends, and warns the response may be cooler than you expect, because many workplaces still assume a man's family life does not touch his hours. The one firm rule is not to leave it late. If you intend to take time off after the birth, shift your hours, or attend appointments during the day, your employer needs months of notice, not weeks. With the due date in June, a conversation early in the new year gives everyone time to plan cover. Decide what you want before you raise it."},
{id:"advice-flood", topic:"you", months:[3,5], title:"The flood of advice and jokes", text:"Once the news is out, expect a steady flow of stories, horror stories, unasked advice, and tired jokes, mostly from other men. Expect people dropping round unannounced. The book's suggestions are practical. Set ground rules early, such as asking people to call before visiting. Treat horror stories as noise. And notice that almost nobody will ask how you are. You have three choices there: ignore it, sulk, or volunteer it yourself, telling friends what has changed for you and what you are nervous about. The third works, particularly with friends who already have children."},
{id:"she-turns-inward", topic:"you", months:[3,4], title:"When she turns inward", text:"Towards the end of the first trimester she will probably spend more time focused on what is happening inside her, worrying whether every twinge means something, wondering if she will be a good mother, and leaning on her own mother and close women friends for models. You may be a trimester behind her and only now getting excited. The risk is that you feel pushed aside and respond by pulling back. The book is firm on this. Do not retaliate by withdrawing. Stay close, and tell her how it feels in a way that is not an accusation. This phase passes."},
{id:"mixed-feelings", topic:"you", months:[3,5], title:"Mixed feelings do not make you a bad father", text:"Most expectant fathers have stretches of being less than thrilled. Fear that something will go wrong, resentment at the cost in time and money, grief for the freedom and the place at the centre of her attention you are about to lose. These feelings usually arrive with guilt attached. The book's advice is to give yourself a break. It also names what deepens the ambivalence: doubts about the relationship, feeling pushed into the pregnancy, unfinished ambitions, money worry, and fear of repeating your own father's mistakes. The reason to notice it is practical. The more ambivalent you stay, the less involved you will be."},
{id:"fifteen-minutes", topic:"you", months:[3,9], title:"Fifteen minutes a day, not about the baby", text:"In one well-known study, 90 percent of couples saw their communication collapse in quantity and quality during the first year with a baby, and for about half it never recovered. The reason is simple. Everything you currently are as individuals, work, friends, interests, gets switched off for a while and replaced by baby talk. The book's remedy is small. Set aside fifteen minutes every day to talk about anything other than the pregnancy or the baby, and start now while life is calm. Fathers who were happier in their relationship before the birth spent more time with their baby in the first year."},
{id:"involved-how", topic:"you", months:[3,6], title:"Say it out loud: how involved you plan to be", text:"One researcher describes three styles of expectant father. The observer keeps an emotional distance and sees himself as a bystander. The expressive father is all in and sees himself as a full partner. The instrumental father manages the pregnancy, scheduling every appointment, meal and workout. None is wrong, but whichever you are, say it to her and ask what she wants. Two findings matter. Her satisfaction with the relationship tracks how involved she perceives you to be, not how involved you feel. And her definition may differ from yours. Earning the money may count as involvement to you and not to her."},
{id:"who-does-what", topic:"you", months:[3,7], title:"Say it out loud: who does what after", text:"How much of the baby's care are you expecting to do? How much is she expecting you to do, and how much does she expect to keep for herself? Couples rarely ask before the birth and then discover their answers differ. The book makes an uncomfortable point. You will be about as involved as she allows. If she wants you hands-on, you will probably want it too. If she wants to keep the care to herself, you will drift to the edge. Research adds that men who take on more of the household and the children feel better about themselves and their family. Have the conversation now."},
{id:"faith-discipline-sleep", topic:"you", months:[3,7], title:"Say it out loud: faith, discipline, sleep, work", text:"The remaining assumptions are the ones couples think are settled because they have never argued about them. How the child will be raised in religion, and whether you both still mean the same thing by that. Whether either of you would ever smack a child, knowing your own upbringing will pull hard here. Where the baby sleeps, in your bed, beside it, or in another room, knowing plans change. How long she plans to be off work, whether you want time too, and what childcare looks like. Whether you need two incomes, and if one is enough, whose. Take one topic a week rather than all at once."},
{id:"hiding-worries", topic:"you", months:[2,9], title:"Why hiding your worries backfires", text:"Men are trained to protect, and during pregnancy protecting her often means shielding her from your own fears, because being strong supposedly means not admitting how much this is affecting you. Two researchers found two costs. You never learn that what you are feeling is normal, because you never say it. And she never gets to find out that you share her feelings, so instead of lowering the stress in the house, your silence raises it for both of you. A further study found men who received emotional support from their partner during pregnancy were in better health and kept stronger relationships."},
{id:"providing-unnoticed", topic:"money-work", months:[4,9], title:"Why providing goes unnoticed", text:"Almost nobody except fathers themselves counts earning the money as involvement. Two fatherhood researchers offer four reasons. Men are assumed to provide, so the effort is taken for granted. Providing happens out of sight, at work, so the family never sees the labour behind it. The provider role carries a stigma, since hours at work read as hours not at home. And people frame work against family as a choice, when most fathers see providing as one part of being a good father. Knowing this will not change how she sees it. It may stop you feeling unappreciated when she does not."},
{id:"debt-saving", topic:"money-work", months:[4,6], title:"Debt first, then automatic saving", text:"Before thinking about long-term funds for the child, deal with the present. High-interest debt undermines everything else, so clearing it comes before or alongside any saving. Then put a fixed amount aside every month, however small, and automate it so tight months cannot quietly cancel it. The book's counter-intuitive rule is to fund your own retirement before the child's education, because education can be borrowed for and retirement cannot. Most of the chapter's detail is American and does not travel. The principles do."},
{id:"life-cover", topic:"money-work", months:[4,8], title:"Life and disability cover, in principle", text:"The book's position is that both of you should have life cover, and that the time to sort it is now. The purpose is that if one of you dies, the other is not forced into a different job just to keep the household running. It argues for simple term insurance over policies that bundle in an investment, because the bundled ones carry heavy commissions. Have someone who is not selling it tell you how much you need before you meet an agent. Long-term disability cover can matter more than life cover, since a long illness is more likely than death. Insuring the baby's life is a waste of money."},
{id:"will-guardian", topic:"money-work", months:[4,8], title:"A will, and who raises the child", text:"The book treats a will as urgent, not optional, and the reason is not the money. It is guardianship. Without a document naming who would raise your child if both of you died, a court decides, and it decides without knowing your wishes. The inheritance rules in the book are American. In the Maldives inheritance follows Shariah principles and a will works differently, so the part to act on is naming a guardian and recording your wishes, with local legal advice on the form. That last sentence is this app's framing, not the book's."},
{id:"baby-month4", topic:"baby", months:[4,4], title:"What the baby is doing at four months", text:"By the end of the fourth month the baby is around 10 to 12 centimetres long and weighs about 110 grams. The heart is fully formed and beats at 120 to 160 a minute, roughly double yours. Fine hair called lanugo covers the body. The baby kicks, swallows and sucks a thumb, swallows more when she eats something sweet and less when it is sour, and turns away from a strong light held to her belly. At the scan, ask the sonographer to describe the movements."},
{id:"small-gestures", topic:"you", months:[3,9], title:"Small gestures that land", text:"Beyond the obvious chores and back rubs, the book's long list has quieter items worth stealing. Hide short notes where she will find them later. Frame the first scan picture. Pack snacks before any outing, because hunger arrives fast. As she grows, pick things up off the floor before she has to bend. Load her phone with the music she would want in the labour room. Service the car and check the tyres before June. Apologise sometimes even when you are not sure you were wrong. Keep a journal of what you are feeling. Tell her she will be a good mother, and mean it."},
{id:"not-to-say", topic:"you", months:[3,9], title:"Things not to say", text:"The book's list is just as useful for what it tells you to avoid. Do not tell her she looks tired or needs a rest, however true. When she says you have no idea what it is like to be pregnant, agree, because you do not. Do not ask whether she just ate or just cried a few minutes ago. If she asks whether she is being unreasonable, or whether something makes her look big, the answer is no. Do not admire a celebrity mother's post-baby figure in her hearing. Do not complain about your own aches. And when she tells you about a bad day, listen without mentioning that yours was worse."},
{id:"loss", topic:"you", months:[2,4], title:"If the pregnancy is lost", text:"The book is honest that miscarriage is common, somewhere between a fifth and a third of pregnancies by some estimates, most in the first three months, and that more than 90 percent of couples who lose one go on to a healthy baby. It is also honest that fathers grieve and are rarely asked how they are. Men tend to grieve stoically and bury themselves in work, which is a way of coping and a way of ignoring it. If it happens, resist trying to fix it or saying you can have another. Ask her what she feels, tell her what you feel, and find at least one person who will ask about you."},

// ---------- The baby's senses, months 5 to 7 ----------
{id:"baby-month5", topic:"baby", months:[5,5], title:"The baby at five months", text:"By the end of this month the baby is roughly 23 cm long and close to half a kilo. Eyebrows and lashes are in, the eyelids still shut, and hiccups have started. She sleeps around 90 to 95 percent of the time, on something like a schedule, and spends the rest kicking, grabbing the cord and turning somersaults. One oddity: an Italian study of dancers who kept performing while pregnant found their babies needed more vigorous rocking to settle. A single study, so a curiosity rather than a rule."},
{id:"touch-first", topic:"baby", months:[4,6], title:"Touch comes first", text:"The senses do not switch on at birth. Each forms early and gets sharper with use. Touch leads. The skin is complete by about two months, and sensitivity starts at the lips and cheeks. Earlier a touch there made the baby turn away. From about now she turns towards it, the same reflex that will later help her find the breast. Palms, soles and the rest follow. Much of her waking time goes on exploring herself, stroking her face, sucking fingers and pushing against the uterine wall."},
{id:"hearing", topic:"baby", months:[4,7], title:"How well the baby hears", text:"The womb is not quiet, and researchers using ultrasound have seen fetuses react to outside sounds from about 14 weeks. At this stage hearing is tilted towards low and middle pitches, which is why an adult male voice carries well. By about 35 weeks the baby picks up high pitches too and can tell them apart. One obstetrician placed a microphone inside the uterus during labour and recorded not only voices and body noise but a symphony playing in the room. Everything reaches her muffled, through fluid and tissue, but it reaches her."},
{id:"heartbeat-sounds", topic:"baby", months:[5,8], title:"Sounds that move the heartbeat", text:"Almost a century ago a German paediatrician noted that fetuses kick when a car horn sounds. Modern studies track heart rate instead: loud fast music tends to raise it, gentle music to lower it, and the mother's voice slows it where a stranger's speeds it up, which researchers read as recognition and calm. The sound she hears most is not a voice but her mother's heartbeat, and newborns settle better to a recording of their own mother's heart than to someone else's. About 80 percent of mothers instinctively hold babies on the left, close to the heart."},
{id:"womb-memory", topic:"baby", months:[6,9], title:"Newborns remember womb sounds", text:"A Belfast researcher found that babies whose mothers followed a particular television serial while pregnant stopped crying at its theme tune. Babies with no exposure did not react. In a much-cited American experiment, sixteen mothers read a chosen story aloud daily in the last six weeks. At three days old, fifteen of the sixteen babies, tested with a dummy that let them choose what they heard by sucking faster or slower, picked the familiar story. Newborns also prefer the language they heard in the womb. The sample sizes are small, but the finding has been repeated in various forms."},
{id:"stimulation-pitch", topic:"baby", months:[5,8], title:"Prenatal stimulation, the sales pitch", text:"Several programmes promise that structured talking, singing or playing heartbeat-like sounds through the belly will produce a calmer, stronger, earlier-talking, higher-IQ child. The book lists these claims at length and then concedes that plenty of researchers find no benefit at all, and the author describes a publicist guaranteeing higher exam scores without being able to say higher than what. Treat the whole category as commercial. Talking to the baby is pleasant and builds your own attachment. Expecting a prodigy sets the child up to disappoint you before she has arrived."},
{id:"bump-rules", topic:"you", months:[5,9], title:"Ground rules for talking to the bump", text:"Your partner's belly is her body, so ask before you start a routine and stop if she finds it intrusive. Speak at normal conversational volume, not a whisper, since the sound has to pass through her. Do it when you actually feel like it, because a flat or bored tone comes through. Keep sessions to fifteen or twenty minutes, at most twice a day, and at roughly the same time so the baby learns something is coming. Pat first to announce yourself. Repeat one song or story but vary the rest, because fetuses tune out anything endlessly repeated."},
{id:"inward-outward", topic:"you", months:[5,6], title:"You go inward, she turns to you", text:"Expect to run about a trimester behind her emotionally. Around now you may become preoccupied with money, safety and the kind of father you will be, sometimes to the point of tuning her out. The trouble is that she has just finished her own inward phase and is now looking outward, at you. She may need reassurance that you are not going anywhere, and brushing that off with a quick everything-will-be-fine tends to make her anxiety grow. Say what is on your mind instead. If her new dependence starts to feel suffocating, tell her gently, and never joke about leaving."},
{id:"sex-middle", topic:"you", months:[4,6], title:"Sex in the middle months", text:"Both heightened desire and none at all are normal for fathers now. Months four to six are often the easiest sexually, since her nausea has passed and increased blood flow can make her more responsive. About a quarter of women feel too awkward or uncomfortable, and some find it painful. Sex does not hurt the baby, who is cushioned in fluid, so try side-lying or her-on-top positions if either of you is anxious. The exception: if she has a history of premature labour, a low-lying placenta or a weak cervix, speak to her doctor first."},
{id:"sex-wrong", topic:"you", months:[4,8], title:"What couples get wrong about sex", text:"Researchers who studied expectant couples found a consistent mismatch: men underestimate how attractive their partners feel, and women underestimate how attracted their partners are. Most men do find the pregnant body appealing, and most women do feel desirable, but neither says so. Men also carry more inhibitions about intimacy during pregnancy than women do, and the idea that pregnancy switches off a woman's desire is not borne out. Couples who talk about sex have more of it, and report happier relationships. Non-sexual affection helps, provided you say up front that is all you mean."},
{id:"baby-month6", topic:"baby", months:[6,6], title:"The baby at six months", text:"About 30 cm long and near a kilo, the baby is still lean, with fat yet to come, and is acquiring a waxy protective coat called vernix. The eyes are starting to open, there are fingerprints and footprints, and the kicks have real force now. If the baby is a girl, her ovaries form their eggs this month. Some researchers place the start of emotional life here, and the book notes that when your partner is stressed, a small dose of the same hormones reaches the baby. That is not a reason to police her moods, only a reason to make her life easier."},
{id:"your-father", topic:"you", months:[6,7], title:"Your father comes back", text:"Many men find that childhood memories of their father arrive unbidden around now, in daydreams and in sleep. One researcher found an expectant father's uncertainty about his new role shows up in dreams that circle back to his own dad. Some memories are warm, some are of fear or absence, and either way you are really asking what kind of father you will be. Research suggests two routes: men who felt well fathered tend to copy the model, and men who did not often set out deliberately to do better. The influence is real. The direction is yours."},
{id:"mortality-roots", topic:"you", months:[6,7], title:"Mortality and family roots", text:"The author describes becoming a slower driver almost overnight once his wife was pregnant, and looking back at his parachuting and diving with horror. Many expectant fathers report the same shift: your death stops being an abstraction because people will depend on you. A related pull is towards family history. Men who were never interested start asking relatives for birth dates, drawing family trees and wanting to know the stories nobody tells. The book frames this as the same impulse that makes us want children in the first place, a wish for some part of us to carry on."},
{id:"small-projects", topic:"you", months:[5,8], title:"Small projects for the months ahead", text:"A few things worth starting now while there is still time. Photograph her belly on a fixed schedule, from the front and side, monthly until the eighth month and weekly after that. Exercise together, and swimming suits her well because the water takes the weight. Put together a physical time capsule, with a newspaper, prices, a list of songs, and whatever else your child will find quaint in twenty years. If a plaster belly cast appeals, use a proper plaster kit only. The book is firm that resin, rubber or plastic casting materials can harm both her and the baby."},
{id:"say-vs-do", topic:"money-work", months:[6,7], title:"What fathers say and do", text:"Surveys of working fathers show a large gap between intention and behaviour. Most say their children are the top priority, most say they would trade pay for family time, yet only a small fraction take more than two weeks off after a birth, and fathers work slightly longer hours than men without children. The book attributes this to social pressure to provide, to unpaid leave, and to a real penalty: men who ask for family leave are judged less committed, mostly by other men. The figures are American and a decade old, but the double bind will feel familiar."},
{id:"flex-case", topic:"money-work", months:[6,8], title:"The case for flexibility", text:"If you need to ask for time off or a changed schedule, go in with a plan rather than a request. Work out with your partner what you ideally need, then propose how the work gets done in your absence and how you will judge whether it is working after a couple of weeks. Acknowledging your employer's needs makes it easier for them to acknowledge yours. Every man the author interviewed who took leave said he would do it again. As a Managing Partner the question is less entitlement than cover, so write the cover plan first."},
{id:"flex-menu", topic:"money-work", months:[6,8], title:"Flexible arrangements, the menu", text:"Flexible work comes in three kinds. Time: shifted hours, a compressed week of four longer days, a different set of days off, or part-time. Place: working from home one or two days a week. Task: sharing a role with a colleague or shedding part of your duties. Two bolder options are becoming a contractor to your current business, and a results-only deal where only output matters. Working from home carries traps: isolation, forgetting to stop, and guilt in both directions. Do not imagine the baby on your lap while you work."},
{id:"baby-month7", topic:"baby", months:[7,7], title:"The baby at seven months", text:"The lungs are maturing, and a baby born now would have a very good chance of surviving. She is 33 to 38 cm, roughly a kilo to a kilo and a half, filling out so the skin is less wrinkled, and getting cramped. The eyes are fully open and the irises respond to light, though it is dim in there and sight will be her weakest sense at birth. Shining a torch at the belly makes her heart race and turn away, so do not. Newborns see best at 25 to 40 cm, about the distance to a face while feeding."},
{id:"feels-real", topic:"you", months:[5,8], title:"When it finally feels real", text:"Fathers accept a pregnancy gradually, and one researcher found many men do not experience the child as real until they hold it, scans notwithstanding. The first kick helps, but it also tends to trigger a wave of feeling unprepared. The author found expectant fathers' dreams track the shift: early on the child appears as a symbol, later as a person, and about half of the fathers he interviewed dreamed of being abandoned. Another difference: ask her to picture herself with the baby and she sees a newborn. Ask yourself and you will probably see a child of three to five, doing something with you."},
{id:"boy-or-girl", topic:"you", months:[4,7], title:"Hoping for a boy or girl", text:"Most couples now learn the sex, and those who do not will hear a hundred infallible folk tests, each exactly as reliable as a coin. Mothers who know they carry a boy describe the kicks as stronger, but measured activity does not differ by sex. Publicly almost everyone says they only want a healthy baby. Privately most have a preference. If you have one, try to drop it, and if you cannot, keep it to yourself for good. Children who learn they were the wrong sex carry it, often into adolescence."},
{id:"naming-traps", topic:"baby", months:[6,9], title:"Naming traps", text:"Say every candidate aloud with the surname, and say the nicknames too, because the nickname is what the playground will use. Write out the initials and check what they spell. Avoid rhymes and puns, and imagine the name on a job application rather than a cot. Decide early whether you are honouring a relative, signalling faith or heritage, or going for something unusual, and whether it will be easy to spell and pronounce where you live and abroad. The names tool under Tools runs the book's ten-each method."},
{id:"family-names", topic:"baby", months:[6,9], title:"Family rules and compromise names", text:"Many families and traditions constrain the choice, from naming the first son after a grandfather to letters tied to the day of birth. If a relative on either side must be honoured for the sake of peace, look for a middle path. The book's example is a president whose parents used a single middle initial standing for two grandfathers at once. It adds a fallback for a couple who cannot agree: one of you names a boy, the other names a girl."},
{id:"classes-leave-out", topic:"birth", months:[6,8], title:"What classes leave out", text:"Classes are standard where they exist, but most instructors address the mother, and the social side, where women compare symptoms, offers you little. Men the author interviewed left feeling like a helper rather than a parent. His fix is to meet the other fathers from the class separately, because men will not raise what actually worries them with their partners present, and research finds men given space to talk end up more involved afterwards. The other lesson classes rarely teach is to ask questions in the delivery room, politely but persistently, every step."},
{id:"methods", topic:"birth", months:[6,8], title:"The main methods compared", text:"The methods differ in how they treat pain. Lamaze began as a technique of overriding pain by concentrating on breathing, and now emphasises informed decisions rather than drug-free birth. Bradley takes the opposite line: let her groan or shout, prepare with diet and exercise, and it gives the father the largest role, though its unmedicated-birth figure comes from the organisation itself. Hypnobirthing descends from the theory that fear causes pain by diverting blood from the uterus, so relaxation reduces it. The McMoyler approach teaches everything that could happen, asks you to trust the medical team, and declares no outcome a failure."},
{id:"drop-coach", topic:"birth", months:[6,9], title:"Drop the word coach", text:"Most methods call the father the coach. The book argues against it. The word squeezes your role into a few hours of labour and ignores the months before and the years after. It casts you as a prop rather than a person going through something hard alongside her. Worst, it implies you should take charge if things go wrong, when twenty hours of classes do not qualify anyone to manage a medical emergency. And everyone knows what happens to coaches when the team loses. If someone calls you coach, the suggested reply is that you are the child's father."},
{id:"pick-class", topic:"birth", months:[6,7], title:"Picking a class together", text:"Classes usually run five to nine weeks and many are available online, so distance is not a bar. Her doctor is the best source of recommendations. Sign up as early as you can, because the knowledge lowers anxiety for the rest of the pregnancy. Before you do, agree between you on a philosophy, including whether she wants medication offered. Fathers who set their hearts on a natural birth, or who push for drugs because they cannot bear her pain, both cause rows. If you disagree, she gets 51 percent of the vote. Fit in an infant first-aid course this month too, because you will not get round to it afterwards."},
{id:"doula-2", topic:"birth", months:[6,8], title:"Doulas, and choosing one", text:"A doula is a trained lay companion who stays through the whole labour supporting both of you. The practice exists in well over a hundred cultures. Studies suggest a doula shortens labour and reduces the odds of pain medication, forceps or a C-section, and the researcher who revived the idea argues a father cannot reasonably be the sole support for twenty hours. Doulas do not deliver babies. If you can find one where your partner will give birth, interview her, check she will bend if plans change, have her meet the medical team, and confirm she sees you as the primary support."},
{id:"not-in-room", topic:"birth", months:[6,9], title:"Not wanting to be in the room", text:"As many as half of expectant fathers feel some reluctance about attending the birth, and the reasons are ordinary: fainting at blood or needles, which affects 10 to 20 percent of adults, fear of losing control, not wanting to watch her suffer, or resenting the pressure. Being forced into a role you cannot stomach helps nobody. The book's advice: talk to other fathers, tell your partner honestly while reassuring her of your commitment, understand she may read reluctance as not caring, take the class anyway, and know that missing the birth itself will not damage your bond, provided you are there straight afterwards."},
{id:"cord-blood", topic:"birth", months:[6,8], title:"Cord blood", text:"Blood and tissue from the umbilical cord are rich in stem cells used to treat leukaemia and several blood and immune disorders. There are two routes: donate publicly, which is free where a hospital collects, or pay a private bank to store it for your own family, which makes most sense with a family history of treatable disease. Collection happens after the birth and is painless for both. Public programmes usually want screening started before 34 weeks, so ask her doctor now whether either option exists where she will deliver. The costs the book quotes are American."},

// ---------- Preparing for after the birth ----------
{id:"recovery-timeline", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"The six-week recovery timeline", text:"Knowing the shape of her recovery before the birth means nothing catches you off guard. Expect vaginal bleeding that fades from red through pink and brown to yellowish over roughly six weeks. Any stitches or a C-section wound will hurt and then ease over a similar period. Constipation and piles are common. Around the third day her breasts fill and feel hard, and if she is feeding, her nipples may be sore for about two weeks. Cramps return while she feeds as the womb shrinks. Hair she kept during pregnancy starts shedding later. Above all, expect deep tiredness."},
{id:"recovery-longer", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Recovery is longer than you think", text:"The book pushes back on the idea that a vaginal birth means a quick bounce back. Recovery after either kind of delivery takes weeks, and women who have had both usually say the vaginal one was easier but not shorter. One study found over 40 percent of new mothers still tired and sore a month on, with sweating, tingling hands and dizziness also common. That is a single study, so treat the figures as indicative. Plan your leave and the help around six weeks, not two."},
{id:"first-feeds", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"How the first feeds work", text:"Her milk does not arrive at birth. For the first two to five days she makes small amounts of colostrum, a thick pre-milk that primes the baby's gut and is all the baby needs. Most of the sucking in those days is practice. Latching is a skill both of them learn from scratch, and cracked or bleeding nipples are common while they do. The book says nipples toughen within about two weeks. It also quotes six or seven feeds a day, which is low by current guidance, so expect eight to twelve. Knowing this beforehand stops you panicking that the baby is starving on day two."},
{id:"your-job-feeding", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Your job during breastfeeding", text:"Research the author cites finds that women whose partners actively back them feed for longer and feel better about it. Backing her means not floating the idea of bottles when week one gets hard. It means fetching food and water while she feeds, saying out loud that she is doing well, and sorting practical help. Ask now who the lactation support is at your hospital or clinic, because many hospitals have someone who can coach her before you leave. The author's own was a male nurse, which tells you how much of this is technique rather than instinct."},
{id:"why-exclusive", topic:"food", months:[7,9], title:"Why exclusive breastfeeding is urged", text:"The American paediatric body recommends nothing but breast milk for six months, then solids phased in over the next six. The book's case covers the baby, with fewer allergies, infections and gut problems and a lower risk of later obesity, and the mother, with faster womb recovery and lower risks of several cancers, diabetes and depression. It adds that breast milk adjusts to the baby's needs. Some of the stronger claims, such as higher IQ, remain contested. The practical points for you are the cost saved and the fact that there is no washing up at 3am."},
{id:"formula", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Formula without the guilt", text:"Some women cannot breastfeed because of low supply, illness, medication or a physical problem, and some decide not to because of work, stress, lack of support or discomfort. Either way she may feel she has failed. Your role has two stages. First help her try everything reasonable to get past the obstacle, including expert help. Then, if it is not working, be the one who says clearly that the baby will thrive on formula and that she has not let anyone down. Decide between you now how you will handle it if feeding does not go to plan."},
{id:"bottle-role", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"A bottle gives you a role", text:"Tucked into a box on twins is a point that applies to any baby. Milk can be expressed faster than a baby can drink it, and a bottle of expressed milk lets the mother rest while you feed. Mothers of twins often choose a mix of breast and bottle for exactly this reason, and fathers of twins end up far more involved in feeding as a result. If feeding is the one thing that pulls the baby toward her and away from you, an occasional expressed bottle is the simplest bridge. Discuss it before birth, and buy a pump only once feeding is established."},
{id:"dressing", topic:"gear", months:[7,9], title:"Dressing a wriggling baby", text:"Newborns have heads too big for their necklines and hands that vanish inside sleeves. Reach in through the cuff and draw the hand out rather than pushing from the shoulder. When you buy clothes, choose ones with poppers running the full length of both legs, because they make nappy changes possible without undressing the baby, and avoid elasticated ankle cuffs. Do not overdress. The rule is the same clothes you are comfortable in, plus a hat, which in Maldivian heat means very little. Shoes are pointless before walking and can harm growing feet."},
{id:"support-head", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Support the head, always", text:"A newborn's head is about a quarter of its body length, compared with a seventh in an adult, and the neck muscles cannot hold it for the first few months. Support it from behind every time you lift, hold or pass the baby, and avoid sudden or jerky movements. Two absolute rules go with this. Never shake a baby, because the brain moves inside the skull and can be permanently damaged. Never throw a baby in the air, however fondly you remember it being done to you. Practise lifts with a doll before the birth if you are unsure."},
{id:"role-first-days", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Your role in the first days", text:"For the first few days you will be lover, friend and father, but the role that matters most is support person for her. She needs time to recover, to get to know the baby, and to learn feeding if that is the plan. How long you are in hospital varies: the author's family spent four days after a C-section and under half a day after a vaginal birth. Either way his first days at home were spent cooking, shopping, washing, finishing the baby's room, spreading the news and screening calls. Set those systems up before the due date so they run without thought."},
{id:"helpers-visitors", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Sorting helpers from visitors", text:"Everyone will ask whether they can help. Some mean it. Others mean they would like to hold the baby for a while and hand her back. The author's method tells them apart instantly: keep a written list of jobs that need doing and ask each person to pick one. Laundry, a shopping run, a repair, a lift to a clinic. The sincere take a task and the rest fade away without offence. Write that list before the baby comes, when you can think straight, and pin it somewhere visible."},
{id:"relatives-move-in", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Relatives who move in", text:"The book is wary of grandparents staying after the birth, which reads oddly where extended family help is the norm and welcome. The useful kernel survives the translation. Older relatives may hold traditional views about a father's place and may not expect or encourage you to do nappies, baths or nights. They will also have firm ideas about feeding, dressing and carrying that may clash with current advice or with your own. Agree with your wife before the birth which decisions are yours as parents, and say so kindly but clearly to whoever is helping. Welcome the hands, keep the final say."},
{id:"tag-team", topic:"after-prep", months:[7,9], title:"Tag-teaming the long cries", text:"You and she do not both have to sit through what one psychiatrist calls the tyranny of crying. Take turns in twenty to thirty minute shifts, and use your off shift to walk or stretch so you come back calmer. If you have tried everything, you can put the baby down for five to ten minutes, then pick her up and try a fresh approach, but only after the gentler responses have failed. If two hours of this has not worked, ring the doctor. Before the birth, write down the numbers you would call when frustration builds, and keep them by the cot."},
{id:"find-fathers", topic:"you", months:[6,9], title:"Find other fathers", text:"You will notice how many groups exist for new mothers and how few for fathers. The author suggests building your own, starting with male friends who already have children: a phone call, a walk with the pushchairs, lunch in a park. He also suggests occasionally asking a male friend to babysit, arguing that a world that never trusts men with children teaches men not to trust themselves. Line up two or three fathers you can talk to before the birth, so the network exists when you need it at week six."},
{id:"gatekeeping", topic:"after-prep", months:[6,9], title:"Maternal gatekeeping", text:"Researchers have a name for the way many mothers quietly limit a father's involvement while saying they want him involved: maternal gatekeeping. Estimates run from a fifth to two thirds of mothers. The author's blunt summary is that you will be as involved as your partner lets you be. It is not malice. Many women are raised to feel that not being the main carer means failing as a mother. Raise this now, gently, so the division of care is a shared decision rather than a drift."},
{id:"family-over-salary", topic:"money-work", months:[7,9], title:"Family time over salary", text:"Survey after survey finds that most men between twenty and forty-five would rather have a work pattern that gives them time with their family than more challenging work or higher pay, and most would trade salary for it. The barrier, the author says, is that the culture around them does not back them. Men who take paternity leave or reduce their hours often find employers question their commitment. This is written about America, but the pattern is familiar. Decide what leave and flexibility you will take, and arrange the cover early."},

// ---------- The first weeks home ----------
{id:"how-she-feels", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"How she may feel inside", text:"Her emotions in the first weeks will be mixed and can change by the hour. Relief that the pregnancy is over sits alongside worry about whether she can mother and feed well. She will want to study the baby intensely, and feel frustrated that her body will not let her move freely. There is often a strange grief at no longer being the one everyone fusses over, and at the plain fact that life has changed for good. Desire is usually low. None of this needs fixing, only noticing and naming. Confidence tends to build on its own over the following weeks."},
{id:"blues-signal", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Baby blues as a signal to you", text:"Up to 80 percent of mothers get a few days of tearfulness and low mood, usually starting a day or two after birth, peaking around day four or five, and lifting within weeks. Most explanations point to hormones or lost sleep. One anthropologist argues instead that the blues track low support, especially from the father, and may work as an unconscious bid for more involvement. That is one researcher's theory, not settled science, but the practical response is the same either way. Take on more of the baby, more of the nights, make sure she eats, and never ask her to snap out of it."},
{id:"if-depression", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"If depression sets in", text:"For roughly 10 to 20 percent of mothers, low mood deepens into depression, sometimes surfacing a month or two after the birth. Many women deny anything is wrong, so if you are worried, arrange the appointment and go with her rather than waiting for her to agree. The book's prescription is specific. Protect one block of at least five hours of unbroken sleep for her each night by taking the baby yourself. Keep telling her the depression is not her fault and that she is doing well. Push her to stop aiming for perfect. And take your own breaks, because a worn-out carer helps nobody."},
{id:"wants-mum", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"When the baby wants mum", text:"Picture this: you are having a lovely time with the baby, she starts to cry, you try everything, and after a few minutes it is obvious she is hungry and only her mother will do. You hand her over and feel useless for the next twenty minutes. The author promises it will sting. The baby is not choosing her over you, she is choosing the food. Do not withdraw. Build a relationship around things that are not feeding, such as bathing, walking, settling and carrying, so you have a channel that is yours."},
{id:"newborn-can-do", topic:"baby", months:[8,9], title:"What a newborn can do", text:"For centuries people assumed newborns could only eat, sleep and cry. In fact a baby a few hours old can copy facial expressions, prefers patterns to plain surfaces and curves to straight lines, and remembers. The author relays a story of an eight-hour-old baby copying a stranger's poked-out tongue and then doing it again when passed back to that same person. That is a story, not a study. The useful part is that babies respond best when alert and awake, that high-contrast black-and-white patterns hold their attention in the early months, and that they will not perform on demand."},
{id:"newborn-looks", topic:"baby", months:[8,9], title:"What a newborn looks like", text:"Expect your baby not to look like the ones in adverts for the first week. A vaginal birth can leave the head slightly pointed and the nose squashed, and both round out over the following months. The first stools are dark green and tar-like, which is normal and clears once feeding starts. Skin can be blotchy, especially on the neck and eyelids, with odd marks or tiny spots that need nothing more than gentle cleaning. Eyes may seem crossed until the muscles coordinate. Fine fuzz on the back and shoulders falls out. The author adds this because disappointment at unmet expectations feeds depression."},
{id:"playing", topic:"baby", months:[8,9], title:"Playing with a newborn", text:"You can play from the first days, but keep each session to about five minutes, because more tires a newborn into fussiness. Start with imitation: poke out your tongue or make a round mouth, hold it, and see if the baby follows. Read the signals. A raised head, bright eyes and a turn toward you mean go on. Crying, squirming, looking away or a glazed look mean stop. Lie on the floor or bed at the baby's level. Keep music low, because newborn ears are sensitive. Do not play boisterously straight after a feed. Choose times when you have no phone in hand."},
{id:"fathers-play", topic:"baby", months:[8,9], title:"Why fathers' play matters", text:"The author summarises research that fathers tend toward physical, high-energy play while mothers lean toward social and emotional play, and that children get something distinct from each. Children who had plenty of fathers' physical play were more popular with peers, more assertive in a healthy way, and better at reading and managing emotions. These are correlations, not proven causes, and the studies are older. The point for you is not to treat your way of playing as the lesser one, even if it looks rougher than hers."},
{id:"boys-girls", topic:"baby", months:[7,9], title:"Boys and girls, treated differently", text:"The book claims inborn differences in how boys and girls respond from the first hours. Much of this draws on popular writing and is contested. The stronger evidence is about adults. In one experiment, two hundred people watched the same video of a nine-month-old startled by a jack-in-the-box. Those told it was a boy saw anger, those told it was a girl saw fear. Mothers respond faster to crying girls, and fathers play more gently with daughters. The advice is simple: cuddle a son as much as a daughter, and later wrestle a daughter as readily as a son."},
{id:"crying-normal", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"How much crying is normal", text:"Around 80 to 90 percent of babies have a daily crying spell of twenty minutes to an hour. Crying is modest in the first fortnight, then climbs, peaking at six to eight weeks before easing. You cannot spoil a newborn by picking her up quickly, and the first time crying stops in your arms you will feel a surge of confidence. Within days you will start to hear different cries for hunger, tiredness, discomfort and plain rage. Babies who are carried more, even when content, cry less overall. Resist handing the baby to her mother by reflex."},
{id:"food-crying", topic:"food", months:[8,9], title:"Food and the crying baby", text:"What she eats can reach the baby through her milk. The author tells a story of evenings of unexplained screaming that turned out to follow a broccoli dinner, which is anecdote, not evidence. More solid is the possibility of a reaction to dairy in her diet, where a doctor may suggest cutting it out for a few days to see what changes. Feeding more often can also help, both because smaller meals are easier on a new gut and because the baby may simply want more closeness. Keep a note of what she eats on bad evenings before changing anything."},
{id:"colic", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Colic explained", text:"About one baby in five develops colic, which differs from ordinary crying by lasting hours at a stretch, sometimes most of a day or night. It typically begins around two weeks, peaks near six weeks, and has usually gone by three months. Nobody agrees on what causes it, so do not expect a doctor to produce a quick fix. Anything given to a baby needs a doctor's go-ahead first. Knowing the three-month horizon in advance is the single most useful thing here."},
{id:"crying-public", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"Crying in public", text:"The author admits that a crying baby in public frightened him more than one at home, because he feared how strangers would judge him. Nobody ever called the police, but there were plenty of comments, from the mildly helpful to the openly sexist suggestion that he take the baby home to her mother. He says almost every father he has asked recognises the feeling, and almost no mother does. Expect it, and decide in advance that a crying baby is not a verdict on you."},
{id:"your-hormones-after", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"Your hormones change too", text:"If you feel oddly calm and reluctant to go back to work, there may be a physical reason. One Canadian researcher found new fathers' testosterone often falls by up to a third after the birth, and other work has found a rise in prolactin, the hormone linked to milk production, even in the trace amounts men carry. The combination appears to make fathers more responsive to a crying baby. These are small studies and the mechanism is speculative, but the finding chimes with what many men describe. The same drop also explains why your own sex drive may dip."},
{id:"jealousy", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"Jealousy, and whom it targets", text:"One psychiatrist calls jealousy the most destructive emotion a new father can carry. The question is who you are jealous of, and the honest answer is usually both of them: her, for being able to feed the baby and for her closeness to him, and the baby, for taking so much of her attention and having free run of a body you are told not to touch. The danger is not in having the feeling but in burying it. Tell her, and if that feels impossible, tell a male friend or relative. You will find the feeling is far more common than anyone admits."},
{id:"pushed-edge", topic:"you", months:[7,9], title:"Feeling pushed to the edge", text:"In the author's research with over a thousand fathers, almost every one described feeling shut out of the early parenting. One nurse researcher found that the mother largely decides whether the father stands in the spotlight or waits in the wings, and that the most inclusive mothers shared what they were feeling and actively invited their partners in. The temptation is to give up and let her get on with it. Instead ask her directly, before the birth, to involve you, and start in the hospital by doing as many nappies and the first sponge bath as you can."},
{id:"engrossment", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"Engrossment and how it starts", text:"One of the oldest studies of fathers and newborns found fathers held, touched, kissed, rocked and talked to their babies at least as often as mothers did. A psychiatrist coined the word engrossment for a father's absorbed fascination with his baby. What switches it on is the same thing that switches it on in women: time in contact with the baby. The more you hold him, the stronger it grows. Fathers who attended the birth bonded slightly faster, but missing the birth does not stop bonding and being there does not guarantee it."},
{id:"slow-love", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"If love is slow to arrive", text:"Two psychiatrists found that between 25 and 40 percent of new mothers and fathers privately describe their first reaction to the baby as indifference. Nobody says so in public. One researcher calls the idea that you must bond in the first fifteen minutes a bill of goods sold to parents. It makes sense: you do not know this person, he may look nothing like you imagined, and the birth may have been frightening. Nearly all fathers report the feeling within two months. Carrying the baby, taking him with you and meeting his daily needs speeds it up."},
{id:"fathers-depression-missed", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"Why fathers' depression gets missed", text:"The book says as many as one in four new fathers show depressive symptoms after the birth, which is the top of the range, with pooled estimates nearer one in ten. Most never seek help. In a British survey, 73 percent of fathers worried about their partner's mental health while only 38 percent thought about their own. Men also express it differently, through anger, withdrawal and long hours at work rather than tears. In one study, people shown matched case descriptions spotted the mother's depression far more often than the father's. Unrecognised, it can still be present nine months later."},
{id:"most-at-risk", topic:"you", months:[7,9], title:"Who is most at risk", text:"The clearest risk factor for a father's depression is a partner who is depressed or has been before, which is one more reason to watch her mood closely. Money worries, a strained relationship with her or with your own parents, and a pregnancy that was unplanned also raise the odds. It affects first-time fathers most. The thin research on effects suggests children of depressed fathers are more likely to have behavioural, emotional and language difficulties. The author's point is that this is a medical condition, not weakness, and that it is treatable. The weekly check-in under Log watches for it."},
{id:"protect-couple", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Protect the couple too", text:"Beyond the housework, the book asks for a few things that are easy to forget. Stop her doing too much too soon, because she will try. Accept that your normal routine is gone for at least six weeks. Make sure you get time alone with the baby while she sleeps or goes out. Keep the relationship between the two of you alive and not only about the child, and when she is ready, go out together and leave the baby with a relative, which in a Maldivian household will be readily available. Be patient with all three of you, and hold on to your sense of humour."},
{id:"sex-after", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Sex after the birth", text:"Doctors usually advise waiting four to six weeks so the cervix can close and bleeding can stop, but that is a guideline, and many couples take much longer. The cliché is that she does not want to and you are desperate. The research says otherwise. The testosterone drop many fathers experience lowers their own interest, and some find the birth images linger. One researcher found the real reasons for low desire were tiredness, stress, lack of time and the baby's sleep, not anything about her body. Talk about it openly rather than circling a date on the calendar."},
{id:"dont-hand-back", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Do not hand the baby back", text:"The first rule for staying involved is to keep hold of the baby. When she reaches to take a crying or smelly baby from you, say that you can handle it or that you need the practice. Ask for advice if you want it, but have her tell you rather than do it for you. Her skill is learned, not innate, and yours will be too. Research finds that lack of opportunity, not lack of feeling, is the main thing stopping fathers being affectionate, and that once holding their babies, fathers coo, rock and soothe as much as mothers do."},
{id:"small-decisions", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Share the small decisions", text:"Involvement is not only cuddles. It means sharing the running decisions of a child's life: planning meals, shopping for food and clothes, booking appointments, knowing the other parents. Leaving these to her signals that you do not think they matter, and taking them on makes it far more likely she will feel comfortable sharing the nurturing role. The author warns against the opposite trap too, where the father becomes only the driver to clinics. Keep some private time with the baby that is not a task. Sorting out who does what before the birth stops the household work quietly sliding onto her."},
{id:"stereotype", topic:"you", months:[7,9], title:"The useless-dad stereotype", text:"The author's first published article counted fathers in children's books and found them almost absent, and where present, distant. A British replication two decades later found little change: fathers were far less likely to appear, to be shown at home, touching their children or expressing emotion. On screen, fathers are portrayed negatively around eight times as often as mothers, usually as well-meaning incompetents who cannot change a nappy. He argues this shapes what girls expect and what boys dare to be. It is worth noticing the books and shows you bring into the house."},
{id:"protects-marriage", topic:"you", months:[7,9], title:"Why involvement protects your marriage", text:"The author's closing argument is that involved fathering is good for the couple, not just the child. Nine in ten new parents suffer a sharp drop in how much and how well they talk in the first year, and for half of them it never recovers. One sociologist found couples who raised children together reported a closer, more stable relationship, and supportive couples coped better with a difficult baby. Involved fathers have partners who are less stressed and less depressed, and markedly lower divorce rates. His instruction is to get involved now and never stop."},
{id:"baby-early", topic:"baby", months:[7,9], title:"If the baby comes early", text:"A baby born before about 37 weeks may need time in an incubator until he can hold his temperature, suck and swallow, breathe unaided, show no infection and reach roughly two kilograms. One thing you can do is massage. One Miami research group found premature babies given three fifteen-minute gentle massages a day gained far more weight and went home nearly a week sooner. That is one group's finding, though widely cited. The technique is long, firm strokes on back and tummy and gentle rolling of limbs, stopping if the baby seems unsettled. Ask the unit before you start."},

// ---------- Months 8 and 9 ----------
{id:"panic-nesting", topic:"you", months:[8,8], title:"Panic and nesting", text:"About six weeks out, many fathers get a jolt: the evenings out, the late nights with friends and the quiet cinemas are about to pause for a long time. The author's response was to go out more in the final two months than in the three years after. The other side is nesting, which research says fathers do as much as mothers, just differently. You may fixate on savings, insurance or a will, or on assembling furniture and painting a room. One researcher calls these the first things a father does for the baby rather than for his partner. Let it run, within budget."},
{id:"cook-double", topic:"food", months:[8,9], title:"Cook double, freeze half", text:"One of the most useful things you can do in the last two months is cook more than you need. Every time either of you makes something that freezes well, double or triple it and pack the surplus in two-person portions. The first week home with a newborn is not a week for chopping onions at nine at night. Thawing a container of curry or sauce takes no thought, and thought is what you will be short of. Label portions with the date. On an island where fresh supplies depend on the boat, a stocked freezer also covers the days when the shop shelves are thin."},
{id:"birth-plan", topic:"birth", months:[7,9], title:"Birth plan: write it, pocket it", text:"The book takes a hard line. Births rarely follow a script, and a list of demands handed to the medical team makes the relationship adversarial before it starts. The suggestion is to do the full exercise together, talking out what you would want in an emergency, on pain relief, on moving around during labour, on photos, on who cuts the cord and when the baby is handed over, and then keep the paper in your pocket as a private cheat sheet. If you do want to share one, keep it short, positive, flexible and polite, show it to her doctor first, and treat it as a conversation starter, not a contract."},
{id:"baby-doctor", topic:"appointments", months:[7,9], title:"Choosing the baby's doctor", text:"You will see this doctor often in the first year: a few days after coming home, two weeks later, then monthly to six months, then at nine and twelve months. Pick someone you can talk to. Ask whether the doctor works with the hospital where the baby will be born, how they feel about vaccination and breastfeeding, how long a routine visit lasts, whether sick and well children share a waiting room, who covers nights and weekends, and whether there is a phone line for the panics that are not emergencies. On an island the last point matters most. Find out now who you ring at 2am and how a referral to Malé works."},
{id:"not-driver", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Don't be the driver", text:"Beyond the basic transport plan, the book makes one point worth holding on to: you should not be the one steering. Nervous drivers get lost, speed and crash, and while your eyes are on the road they are not on her. Arrange for someone else to take the wheel, or the tiller, and have two or three backups because people sleep through phones at 2am. The same logic applies to a speedboat or a transfer to Malé. Sit with her. Also put the doctor's number in both your phones, keep spare keys somewhere known, and hand over urgent work to a colleague now, because labour can run past a day."},
{id:"cot-needs", topic:"gear", months:[6,9], title:"What a cot needs", text:"A borrowed or family cot needs a hard look before the baby goes in it. Corner posts are a strangulation risk because clothing can catch on them, so new cots do not have them and old ones should have them removed. Gaps between bars should be no more than about 6 cm, none missing or cracked. Drop-side cots, where one side lowers, were banned in some countries after thousands of injuries, so refuse one even as a gift. Keep the cot away from curtain and blind cords, heaters and loose rugs. Check that any plastic teething rail on the top edge is firmly fixed, because it will be chewed."},
{id:"firm-mattress", topic:"gear", months:[6,9], title:"Firm mattress, nothing soft", text:"The mattress is usually sold separately. Buy a good firm one with a waterproof cover, because a soft surface raises the risk of cot death and a decent mattress outlasts one child. It must fit tightly, with no more than a finger's width between mattress and frame. Then keep the cot bare. No pillows. No bumper pads, including the mesh kind sold as breathable, because babies can wedge between pad and mattress or press their faces in and not back away. A few bumps against the bars are the safer trade. If you want a mobile, choose high-contrast patterns and remember the baby sees it from below."},
{id:"where-sleeps", topic:"gear", months:[6,9], title:"Where the baby sleeps at first", text:"Paediatric guidance quoted in the book is for the baby to sleep in your room, but not in your bed, for the first six months, which may cut the risk of cot death by as much as half and makes night feeds far easier. A bassinet or Moses basket does that job, but it is outgrown by about six months, so borrow one and pass it on rather than buying. Apply the same checks as a cot: no posts, bars too close for a drinks can to pass through, a firm snug mattress, and respect the maker's weight limit. A three-sided sidecar cot that clips to her side of the bed is another option."},
{id:"car-seat", topic:"gear", months:[7,9], title:"Car seat", text:"In the US a hospital will not discharge a baby without one, which is why the book calls it the one essential purchase. Your situation differs: if your island has no cars, you may need one only for the Malé leg or taxis. Where you will use one, do not skimp. Avoid second-hand seats unless they are nearly new, have never been in a collision and meet current standards, because you cannot see internal damage. Infant seats with a carry handle last to about 9 kg. Practise fitting it before the birth, and dress the baby in something with legs so the harness goes between them."},
{id:"changing-station", topic:"gear", months:[7,9], title:"Changing station and small kit", text:"Any waist-high surface with a foam pad and two washable covers will do. Set it up so everything is within reach before you start, because a wriggling baby cannot be left while you hunt for a clean vest. Stock it with nappies, wipes, barrier cream, and cotton buds and water for the cord stump. Skip talc and cornstarch powders entirely, both are linked to breathing problems and neither prevents rash. Changing often and letting the skin dry does. Elsewhere keep a forehead or ear thermometer, a bulb for clearing a blocked nose, baby nail scissors and a small first aid kit your doctor has approved."},
{id:"pushchair-carrier-bath", topic:"gear", months:[7,9], title:"Pushchair, carrier, bath", text:"For a pushchair the book's advice is to ignore the extras and judge weight, one-handed folding, brakes, balance so it cannot tip backwards, and handle height. Most are built for women, so if you are tall check you can push without stooping. On sandy or uneven island paths, wheel size will matter more than anything else. A carrier, whether sling or front pack, should be comfortable and adjustable if you will both wear it. For bathing, a small plastic basin beats the kitchen sink: newborns get sponge baths lying down, and you want two hands free for a slippery baby. A monitor is optional in a small house."},
{id:"clothes-nappies", topic:"gear", months:[7,9], title:"Clothes and nappies: don't overbuy", text:"Newborn sizes are outgrown within weeks, so buy few of them and skip the size entirely if scans say the baby is big. The book's rough wardrobe: five or six bodysuits, a few vests, three or four two-piece outfits with poppers so you only wash the dirty half, three or four sleepsuits, four or five blankets, a couple of hooded towels, and no shoes until walking. Everything will be covered in milk or worse daily, so choose washable fabrics and wash new clothes before first wear. Nappies: a newborn uses about a dozen a day, so a week's stock at home is the minimum."},
{id:"cloth-disposable", topic:"gear", months:[7,9], title:"Cloth or disposable", text:"The book declines to crown a winner. Disposables fill landfill. Cotton is thirsty to grow, and a laundry service washes each nappy many times in near-boiling water. Call it a draw and decide on practicality. Modern cloth nappies are shaped, with Velcro covers, and leak no more than disposables if fitted well. Hybrids pair a washable outer with a throwaway liner. One claim worth noting: children in disposables tend to toilet train later because they never feel wet. For you the deciding factors are likely to be laundry water and what happens to waste on your island, so check both before buying in bulk."},
{id:"month9-body", topic:"her-body", months:[9,9], title:"Her ninth month: body and mood", text:"Expect kicks to turn into squirming, because there is no room left. When the baby's head drops into the pelvis she may breathe and eat more easily and get a burst of energy, but cramps, backache, constipation and swelling of feet, ankles and face continue. Her weight may plateau or dip. Interest in sex usually fades, though not for everyone. Emotionally she may lean on you more than ever, fear you will love her less once she is a mother, be fed up with “any news?” questions, worry she will not have enough love to share, and write long lists of jobs, mostly for you."},
{id:"baby-month9", topic:"baby", months:[9,9], title:"Baby in the final month", text:"Through the last month the baby gains roughly 100 to 225 grams a week, then stops growing about a week before birth. At term most weigh between 2.7 and 4 kg and measure around 50 cm, folded up tight. Fingernails and toenails are often long enough to need trimming straight after birth. The fine body hair and the waxy coating that protected the skin are starting to shed. Sight is developing normally, despite the old myth that newborns cannot see. In these weeks the baby rehearses sucking, swallowing, blinking, clenching fists, turning the head and even breathing movements, all needed within minutes of arrival."},
{id:"contradictory-month", topic:"you", months:[9,9], title:"Your contradictory last month", text:"The book calls the final month the most confusing for fathers, because every feeling of the past eight months returns, often in pairs that do not match. Confident about fatherhood yet unsure you can hold down work, marriage and a baby at once. Keen to be involved yet jealous of her bond with the baby. Closer to her than ever, with no sex life. Giddy about holding your child, afraid of dropping him. Many men also start blaming themselves for what she is going through. She does not blame you. This was a joint decision and there is no other route to a baby, so spend that energy on something useful."},
{id:"final-kindnesses", topic:"you", months:[9,9], title:"Small kindnesses, final weeks", text:"She will be uncomfortable and there is little you can fix, but you can lighten the load. Stop fielding the “has it happened yet” calls with a recorded message. Come home earlier, postpone trips, and keep your phone on you always. Send a couple of short messages a day. Stay calm, and be patient with odd requests, even cleaning the house a third time. Practise the breathing you plan to use. If she is still working, encourage her to stop three or four weeks before the date: one Californian study found those who did had a far lower C-section rate. Single study, but rest costs nothing."},
{id:"overdue", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Due dates and going overdue", text:"A due date is a guess with a two-week window either side, assuming a 28-day cycle. A first-trimester scan narrows it to three or four days, later scans less so. Only about 5 percent of babies arrive on the day and first babies often run up to ten days late, so 13 June 2027 means most of June, and she should hear that from you before she fixes on the date. Truly overdue brings risks: a large baby, an ageing placenta, falling fluid. Expect a scan and heart-rate test every few days, then talk of induction. One study links heat to births about six days earlier on average, relevant to June here."},
{id:"labour-length", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"How long labour really takes", text:"Start to placenta, the book puts a typical labour at twelve to twenty-four hours, longer for first babies, and admits there are no firm rules. Early labour is the long part, averaging around eight hours but running to days, mostly at home, with contractions of 30 to 45 seconds up to twenty minutes apart. Active labour is given as three to five hours, transition one to two, pushing about two, the placenta under twenty minutes. Treat these as rough. Averages hide how often first labours run well past them, and the author's own wife had hours of regular contractions daily for a week beforehand. Plan island transport for the slow version, not the film version."},
{id:"early-labour-home", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Early labour at home", text:"While contractions are mild, your job is comfort and record keeping. Time every contraction, how long and how far apart, because the doctor will ask before telling you to come in. The timer under Tools does this. Ask her regularly what would help and then do it. Many doctors allow light food in early labour and water is essential. You should eat and drink too, and both of you should rest, because adrenaline will fool you into thinking you can last. You will know labour has turned active when she can no longer walk and talk through a contraction and stops caring about modesty. If she says it is time, go. On an island, go earlier."},
{id:"transition", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Transition: whatever she wants", text:"In the last stretch before pushing, contractions come every two to three minutes and last up to ninety seconds. She may be shaking, vomiting and frightened. Television has prepared you for her to turn on you. The author's wife never did, though she did ask him to leave the bathroom for a while. If yours snaps, swears or sends you out, do it at once and say you are just outside. If she says the dark room is too bright, find something to switch off. Do not argue, do not reason, and do not sulk. Her mind is wholly occupied with getting through each contraction and even a kind touch can distract. Offer ice chips instead."},
{id:"learn-room", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Arriving: learn the room", text:"On arrival, spend the first minutes finding where flannels, gowns, blankets and extra pillows are kept, where the stool for you is, where the shower, the call button and the bed controls are, and where ice and warmed blankets live. Nurses are often stretched and you will want these without waiting. Then introduce yourself to the staff by name. Tell them about her allergies, any fear of needles, any past bad experience, and say plainly that you want to be involved and would welcome their guidance. A warm relationship with the team changes the whole day."},
{id:"breath-sound", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Coaching her breath and sound", text:"The author's co-writer, a childbirth educator, dismisses the patterned breathing taught in many classes and screaming alike. What works is slow breathing, five seconds in and five out, and low guttural moaning through each contraction with real rest in between, the sound you would make lifting something heavy. Noise is fine, the next room is making its own. Watch for clenched jaw, fists, hunched shoulders and held breath, because tension makes everything worse. Early on, get close, hold her gaze and tell her directly what to release. Later, stop talking and show her instead, letting your own body go slack and breathing with her."},
{id:"touch-movement-words", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Touch, movement, words", text:"Women with actively supportive partners tend to have shorter labours and report less pain, so your presence is not decorative. Offer massage of back, hands or feet, and when massage irritates, try firm sustained pressure instead, asking whether higher on the back, lower, or near the tailbone helps most. Keep water, ice chips and a cold cloth to hand, and make sure she empties her bladder every hour, which also tells you whether she is drinking enough. Get her upright and moving when she can, because gravity works better that way. Walk through contractions early, between them later. Tell her she is doing it, often. From you it carries."},
{id:"pushing-birth", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Pushing and the moment of birth", text:"When pushing starts the room changes. More staff arrive, equipment appears, and the nurses take over the coaching, because recognising a productive push is a skill learned over years. You may feel sidelined after hours of being central. Stay at her side anyway, not behind a camera, and ask how you can help. If you want to receive the baby as she comes out, arrange it before crowning, not during. Do not expect a clean pink infant. Newborns arrive coated in white wax, often bluish, bloody, with puffy eyes, swollen genitals, downy shoulders and a head shaped like a cone from the journey. It passes."},
{id:"placenta", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"The placenta", text:"The birth is not finished when the baby is out. Within five minutes to an hour, with mild contractions she may barely notice, the placenta separates and is delivered, and any tear is repaired. Most parents are too absorbed in the baby to register this stage. The book spends several pages on what cultures do with the placenta and notes that hospitals in some places restrict what you can take home. If your family has a custom for it, ask the hospital beforehand rather than on the day. Otherwise it is examined and disposed of as medical waste, and you need do nothing."},
{id:"pain-decide-together", topic:"birth", months:[7,9], title:"Pain relief: decide together first", text:"The book argues popular guides understate labour pain. She is the one hurting, so her judgement leads, but as labour deepens she will be less able to decide, which makes you her advocate. Agree beforehand whether she wants you to raise medication if you see her suffering or to wait until she asks, and settle a code word that means she truly wants it, since some women shout for relief without meaning drugs. One Italian study found fathers felt three times more useful when their partner had an epidural. If relief leaves you feeling less needed, find another way to help."},
{id:"epidural", topic:"birth", months:[7,9], title:"The epidural explained", text:"She curls on her side or leans forward, the lower back is numbed, and an anaesthetist threads a fine tube into the space around the spinal cord and tapes it in place. It works within minutes and dulls contraction pain while leaving her awake. The book says it does not reach the baby, which is a simplification, so ask the anaesthetist. Older worries about longer labours and more C-sections have faded as dosing improved. For about one woman in ten the block is patchy or one-sided. Ask whether your hospital offers it at all, around the clock, and whether patient-controlled dosing exists."},
{id:"other-drugs", topic:"birth", months:[7,9], title:"Other drugs and the baby", text:"Besides the epidural there are two less common regional options: a spinal, a single injection that works faster but wears off sooner, and a pudendal block that numbs only the vaginal opening. The other family is systemic drugs, opioids and sedatives given by injection or drip. They can be used from the start of labour, take the edge off quickly and ease anxiety, but they make her drowsy and do more to make her stop caring than to remove pain. They also cross to the baby, who may be sleepy or slow to suck for a while. The effects wear off and there is a reversal drug. General anaesthesia is for emergency C-sections."},
{id:"induction", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Induction: how, when, not for convenience", text:"From 40 weeks the baby is fully developed, and if the due date is well past, or labour has stalled and she is exhausted, the doctor may propose starting or restarting it. Usually that means a gel or tablet to soften the cervix, then a synthetic form of the contraction hormone through a drip, often alongside an epidural so she can rest before pushing. Whether this makes labour more painful is disputed. Folk remedies abound, from spicy food to sex, and the book says check with the doctor first. What it opposes firmly is inducing early for convenience: the baby's brain nearly doubles in size in the final weeks."},
{id:"forceps-vacuum", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Forceps and vacuum", text:"If she is fully dilated and has pushed a long time without the baby moving, or the baby is in distress, or she is too tired or too numb to push well, the doctor may help the head out. Forceps are curved blades that cradle the head. They are now used in roughly half of one percent of births and can leave bruising on the baby's face for a few days. A vacuum cup on the scalp does the same job in a few percent of births, with some scalp swelling. Most doctors are trained in one or the other. Either can spare her a C-section."},
{id:"episiotomy", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Episiotomy", text:"An episiotomy is a cut between the vagina and the anus to widen the opening as the head comes through. Decades ago most American births included one, on the theory that a clean cut healed better than a tear. Research found the opposite, that the cut can extend into a worse tear and raises the risk of bowel, bladder and sexual problems afterwards, so most hospitals now do far fewer. About 70 percent of first-time mothers tear a little on their own and that heals better. It is still justified for a very large baby, forceps, breech, or when many tears are clearly coming. Ask her doctor's rate."},
{id:"monitoring", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Fetal monitoring", text:"Most hospital labours involve two belts around her belly, one tracking the baby's heartbeat and one her contractions. Fathers tend to like the screen because it gives them a role, but women often find it a nuisance and the trace lags reality, so never announce that a big one is coming. Without an epidural she can ask for intermittent checks with a handheld Doppler. With an epidural, a drip or during pushing, continuous monitoring is standard. The book's case for less: doctors agreed on only 22 percent of traces in one study, and continuous monitoring raised C-section rates without cutting cerebral palsy. Turn the sound down."},
{id:"first-minutes", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"First minutes: cord and checks", text:"You may be offered the cord to cut, and one Portuguese study found fathers who did were more involved with the baby a month later. At one minute and again at five, a nurse scores colour, pulse, reflexes, movement and breathing from 0 to 2 each. Most babies land between 7 and 9 and almost none gets 10. Then come weighing, measuring, an identity band, a wipe-down, a nappy and, in many places, antibiotic eye ointment. All of this should happen within the hour, but you can ask for a few minutes of skin contact first. Most newborns are not hungry for around twelve hours, so an early try at the breast is about contact, not feeding."},
{id:"csection-your-side", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"C-section from your side", text:"Most fathers feel relief when a C-section is called, 92 percent in one study, yet it is hard on you too. Expect to be sent out while she is prepared, often told nothing, and to wait in a corridor. Inside you are seated by her head behind a screen, and the stated reason is practical: this is when fathers faint. The baby is usually taken straight off to have fluid suctioned from the lungs. Tell her what is happening. If the baby leaves the room, ask to go too and take your shirt off. A Swedish study found babies on a father's bare chest stopped crying within fifteen minutes and settled far sooner than those left in a cot."},
{id:"csection-medical", topic:"birth", months:[8,9], title:"Medical reasons for a C-section", text:"Most are not planned. Common triggers: labour that stops progressing despite good contractions or leaves her too exhausted to push, a pelvis that proves too small, which cannot be known until she tries, a baby whose heart rate drops or traces badly, twins, a placenta coming away early or lying over the cervix, and a baby lying bottom-first or sideways. Only 3 to 4 percent of single babies are breech, and doctors usually try turning them by hand first. Her own conditions count too, such as high blood pressure, diabetes or heart disease. A baby over about 4.5 kg raises the risk of the shoulders sticking."},
{id:"csection-nonmedical", topic:"birth", months:[7,9], title:"Non-medical reasons for a C-section", text:"The book is blunt that not every C-section is necessary. Rates swing from under 25 percent to over 40 percent between American states and from 35 percent in Thailand to 70 percent in Brazil, which cannot all be medicine. Some women choose one to pick a date or to avoid the pain and pelvic floor effects of a vaginal birth, which surgery does prevent, at the cost of around six weeks when she cannot climb stairs, drive or lift the baby without pain. Auspicious dates, a doctor's wish to be available, fear of being sued and financial incentives all push rates up. Ask your hospital its rate and how it decides, before you need to know."},
{id:"csection-body", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Her body after a C-section", text:"It is major abdominal surgery. The wound will be painful for several days, managed at first through a drip. Nurses will come often to press on her belly to check the uterus is firming. The drip stays until her bowels restart, usually within a day, then fluids, soft food, normal food. Within 24 hours they will have her up and shuffling a few steps, which looks brutal and is necessary. The incision may be closed with stitches, staples, glue or tape. At home she will need pads for weeks, loose clothing, wound supplies, and someone to do all the lifting. That is you."},
{id:"csection-mind", topic:"after", months:[8,9], title:"Her mind after a C-section", text:"The book explains why your words carry particular weight. An unplanned C-section, especially one for “failure to progress”, leaves many women replaying their decisions, wondering whether another hour would have changed things, and feeling they fell short. Left alone those thoughts can feed postnatal depression. She needs to hear, specifically, that she could not have done more, that she did not give in early, that she tried everything to restart a stalled labour, and that agreeing to surgery was the right call for the baby and for her. Say it anyway, because you were the one in the room, and that makes it land differently than from a nurse or a relative."},
{id:"frightening-birth", topic:"you", months:[8,9], title:"When the birth was frightening", text:"Your pulse will climb anyway, from around 72 to 115 beats a minute just before birth in one Scandinavian study. But if there was a genuine emergency, the mix of not knowing what was happening, not knowing whether she and the baby were alive, and being unable to help can leave a father with post-traumatic stress, according to Oxford research. Men rarely seek help, fearing they will look weak or that their needs should not count. Untreated, flashbacks and withdrawal make it harder to bond with the baby and to support her. If you recognise this in yourself in the weeks after, talk to someone. Her memory of the pain fades within months. Yours may not."}

];
